  data: {
    LLM: "Keep track of the numbers. If you have an 8, and there's a 5 and a 3 on the table, take them immediately to score more cards. Don't trail high cards (Aces/Kings) unless you have no other choice, as they are likely to be captured by opponents in the next turn.\n\nThe 'Sauterelle' Denial: If you see the table is down to just a few cards that could be easily summed up (like a 2, 4, 1), and it's almost your opponent's turn, try to capture just ONE of those cards, even if it's less efficient. This 'breaks the chain' and prevents them from getting the Grasshopper bonus.\n\nStamina Management: In Papillon, being the first to empty your hand confers a bonus. If you have cards that can capture combinations, use them late. Early in the hand, focus on single-card matches (like Jack for Jack) to keep your combination-options open for when the table becomes more crowded.",
    Player: "Keep track of the numbers. If you have an 8, and there's a 5 and a 3 on the table, take them immediately to score more cards. Don't trail high cards (Aces/Kings) unless you have no other choice, as they are likely to be captured by opponents in the next turn.\n\nThe 'Sauterelle' Denial: If you see the table is down to just a few cards that could be easily summed up (like a 2, 4, 1), and it's almost your opponent's turn, try to capture just ONE of those cards, even if it's less efficient. This 'breaks the chain' and prevents them from getting the Grasshopper bonus.\n\nStamina Management: In Papillon, being the first to empty your hand confers a bonus. If you have cards that can capture combinations, use them late. Early in the hand, focus on single-card matches (like Jack for Jack) to keep your combination-options open for when the table becomes more crowded.",
